lesson.body:
The In Operator for Lists chapter showed that x in some_list works by scanning the list from the front until it finds a match or reaches the end. That works fine for a handful of items, but for a list with a million entries, checking membership might mean examining all one million of them in the worst case. Sets, introduced in the Sets chapter, are built specifically to make this kind of check fast, and the in operator works on sets using the exact same syntax you already know from lists.

The reason sets are faster comes down to how they store data. A list keeps elements in the order you put them in and has no shortcut for finding a specific value except checking each one. A set instead uses each element's hash value to decide roughly where to store it internally, the same mechanism frozensets and dictionary keys rely on. To check membership, Python computes the hash of the value you're looking for and jumps almost straight to where it would be, instead of scanning everything. This makes in on a set run in roughly constant time on average, regardless of how many elements the set holds, compared to time proportional to the list's length for in on a list.

allowed_ids = {4521, 7788, 9902, 1234}
user_id = 7788

if user_id in allowed_ids:
    print("Access granted")
else:
    print("Access denied")

This prints Access granted, since 7788 is one of the four values stored in allowed_ids. The syntax looks identical to checking membership in a list, but if allowed_ids held a hundred thousand IDs instead of four, this check would still run about as fast, while the equivalent list version would slow down as the list grows.

This speed difference is exactly why the Removing Duplicates chapter, later in this batch, and many real programs convert a list to a set before doing repeated membership checks: build the set once, then test membership against it as many times as needed, and each test stays cheap. The tradeoff is that sets, unlike lists, do not preserve order and cannot hold duplicate or unhashable values, so you choose a list or a set depending on whether order and repeats matter or whether fast lookup matters more.
